@@ -0,0 +1,67 @@
+# User-request lifecycle
+
+A native run can process several admitted requests.
+Use request boundaries when observation must cover exactly one request response, including its tools, retries, recovery, and automatic continuations.
+An explicitly selected `all` queue batch has one response scope containing every admitted request in that selection.
+
+`UserRequest`, `UserRequestGroup`, and `UserRequestScope` are exported by agent-core and coding-agent.
+It has readonly `{id:string,source:string,delivery:"prompt"|"steer"|"followUp",correlationId?:string}` fields.
+Native IDs are fresh UUIDs allocated before input transforms and retained through queue admission and consumption.
+They are independent of prompt text and timestamps.
+Native `InputEvent.request` supplies the same provenance to every chained input handler.
+The declaration permits extension-constructed input events without provenance; native session input always supplies it.
+The ID and source remain stable if asynchronous input transformation changes the session busy state.
+Input delivery describes planned routing; admission and start records describe the actual route without creating another ID.
+Before an idle `prompt()` executes input transforms, expansion, or hooks, Pi awaits `user_request_prepare` with `{type:"user_request_prepare",request:UserRequest}`.
+This is an observation interval, not queue admission or a started model response.
+`ctx.getUserRequestPreparation()` returns its original request until it transfers to an actual response or ends.
+Its optional declaration preserves externally constructed legacy contexts; native preparation-capable contexts always supply it.
+Integrations needing first-edit coverage must check this capability as well as `getUserRequest()`.
+Busy input does not create another preparation interval or change the active response scope.
+The first actual response inherits any outstanding idle preparation interval without resampling its baseline.
+If asynchronous input changes which request starts first, `UserRequestStartEvent.preparationId` explicitly identifies that original interval for binding to the selected native scope.
+Normal same-ID start is idempotent, and an intentional `all` selection still starts exactly one group response.
+Handled input or rejected preflight ends an unconsumed interval through awaited `user_request_prepare_end` with `{request,outcome:"completed"|"interrupted"|"failed"}`.
+Handled extension work is completed, abort is interrupted, and rejected preflight is failed; these notifications do not manufacture model responses.
+Observation notifications cannot transform input or request continuation, and input handlers retain their ordinary ordering and error policy.
+Extension commands still execute before ordinary prompt interception, and queue-only `steer()`/`followUp()` calls retain their upstream admission semantics.
+
+`user_request_start` and `user_request_end` are awaited extension notifications.
+Payloads are `{type:"user_request_start",request}` and `{type:"user_request_end",request,outcome}`.
+Outcomes are `completed`, `interrupted`, and `failed`.
+Start finishes before selected-response preparation, provider work, and tools.
+For idle prompt input work, observe the earlier preparation barrier too.
+End finishes after tools, retries, recovery, and automatic continuations, before the next distinct request starts work.
+A terminal unrecovered provider or runtime failure is failed; abort or interactive steering superseding an active scope is interrupted.
+Already executing tools finish at their ordinary steering boundary.
+Notification handlers cannot return continuation decisions; ordinary handler errors are reported and coding can continue.
+
+`UserRequestScope` is `UserRequest | UserRequestGroup`.
+A group has readonly `{id:string,mode:"all",requests:readonly UserRequest[]}` fields.
+Its fresh response-scope UUID is independent of every member UUID; the ordered members retain their original source, delivery, and optional correlation reference.
+A group represents one actual response addressing an intentional batch, rather than separate completed responses for its members.
+The lifecycle event `request` and `ctx.getUserRequest()` and `session.userRequest` use this scope union.
+The context getter and session property return undefined outside a response.
+The context method is optional for extension-created contexts; the native runtime supplies it.
+Session subscribers observe both events using their existing notification-only contract.
+
+`enqueueMessage()` retains synchronous native queue admission and the exact `{status:"queued"}` receipt.
+It creates a fresh extension-origin request even when idle and no input event occurs.
+Its optional `correlationId` admission option retains a submitter-owned reference without replacing the native ID or guaranteeing deduplication.
+Repeated correlation references still create distinct requests.
+Custom messages triggering work create requests; context-only custom messages, nextTurn messages, boundary entries, retries, and internal continuations stay in their owning response.
+
+General `AgentMessage.userRequest` metadata and session-owned `pi.user-request` custom entries carry provenance.
+Custom entries record admitted/start/end phases, exclude prompt text, and do not participate in model context.
+User-message metadata survives ordinary persistence; custom-message admission and lifecycle provenance live in custom entries without changing caller details.
+Queues remain memory-only, and admission records do not prove consumption or successful completion.
+
+Queue selection, order, and model-facing batching follow the configured SDK modes unchanged.
+Ordinary one-at-a-time selection starts one scope per admitted request.
+Explicit `all` selection with two or more distinct tagged requests starts one group scope and publishes one actual group outcome.
+Selection with one tagged request uses its individual scope; untagged context contributions do not create group members.
+Start finishes before preparation for the entire selection, and its baseline remains active throughout tools, retries, recovery, and automatic continuation.
+A new selected group interrupts a superseded steering scope in the same way as a single steering request.
+AgentSession uses the general agent-core `beforeInput` barrier for selected input and `deferFollowUp` scheduling option to perform recovery and settlement before consuming follow-ups.
+Direct agent-core consumers keep default scheduling unless they select these options.
+Steering remains tool-safe and supersedes an active scope rather than becoming an independent completed follow-up.
